{{also|move the goal posts}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|move the goal posts}}

===Etymology===
Metaphor derived from [[association football]], of British origin<ref>{{cite-web
|date=October 28, 1990
|url=http://www.nytimes.com/1990/10/28/magazine/on-language-moving-the-goalposts.html
|title=On Language; Moving the Goalposts
|author=William Safire
|work=New York Times}}</ref>.

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb|head=[[move]] the [[goalpost]]s}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[alter]] the [[agreed]] [[target]] or [[rule]]s of [[negotiation]]s or of an [[agreement]] while it is ongoing, especially in an unfair way.
#: {{ux|en|This is a Government that meets its economic targets and does not '''move the goalposts'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We were given a moving date, and an exchange date, but the buyers kept '''moving the goalposts''' and giving us a later date.}}

